---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const b11 = requireSection('B1.1');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    B1 sets up the problem-solving toolkit used throughout the rest of the course. The chapter
    begins with defining a problem clearly, then moves through the main computational-thinking
    concepts, and ends by tracing flowcharts as a way of checking algorithmic logic.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Start by asking what the problem actually is, who it affects, and how success will be judged.
      Then reduce the problem through abstraction, decomposition, and pattern recognition before
      checking the final logic with a flowchart trace.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b11}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B1.1.1"
    summary="Construct a problem specification by defining the problem, its stakeholders, its limits, and the criteria for judging success."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A problem specification is a short, precise definition of what needs to be solved and why. The
      command term matters here: to construct a problem specification means producing one, not
      only listing the parts it should contain.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-problem-specification">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">What it should make clear</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Problem statement</td>
          <td>What the issue is, who it affects, and what is currently going wrong</td>
          <td>Keeps the solution focused on a real need</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Stakeholders</td>
          <td>Who uses, supports, manages, or is affected by the system</td>
          <td>Different users often have different priorities</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Constraints and limitations</td>
          <td>Time, budget, hardware, legal, ethical, or staffing limits</td>
          <td>Prevents unrealistic planning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Inputs and outputs</td>
          <td>What data enters the system and what the system must produce</td>
          <td>Clarifies the required data flow</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Evaluation criteria</td>
          <td>Specific, measurable tests of success</td>
          <td>Allows the final solution to be judged properly</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-drafting-a-problem-specification">Worked example: drafting a problem specification</h3>
    <p>
      Suppose a school canteen wants to replace paper lunch orders with a digital kiosk system.
      A strong problem specification would identify students, catering staff, and school managers as
      stakeholders, note the budget and allergy-reporting requirements as constraints, and define
      inputs and outputs clearly.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Part</th>
          <th scope="col">Example for the canteen system</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Problem statement</td>
          <td>Lunch queues are too slow and spoken orders sometimes lead to mistakes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Constraints</td>
          <td>Limited budget, allergy rules, and the need to continue working during network problems.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Inputs</td>
          <td>Touchscreen choices, student identification, allergy data.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Outputs</td>
          <td>Confirmed meal order on screen, kitchen order ticket, and updated queue record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Evaluation criteria</td>
          <td>Queue time below five minutes and no recorded mismatch between order and meal.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B1.1.2, B1.1.3"
    summary="Describe the fundamental computational-thinking concepts and explain how they are applied across computer-science problem solving."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Computational thinking is a toolkit for solving problems in a structured way. It is not the same
      as programming itself. Instead, it shapes how a computer scientist approaches a problem before
      implementation begins.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-computational-thinking-concepts">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Definition</th>
          <th scope="col">Useful effect</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Abstraction</td>
          <td>Focus on the essential features and ignore irrelevant detail</td>
          <td>Makes complex systems manageable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Decomposition</td>
          <td>Break a complex problem into smaller sub-problems</td>
          <td>Supports planning and parallel work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pattern recognition</td>
          <td>Identify similarities that allow reuse of ideas or methods</td>
          <td>Reduces repeated effort</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Algorithmic thinking</td>
          <td>Design a clear, ordered sequence of steps to solve the problem</td>
          <td>Produces predictable and testable solutions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        Decomposition means breaking down the problem, not breaking down a finished program.
        At the point decomposition happens, the code may not exist yet.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="applied-understanding-computational-thinking">Applied understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Area</th>
          <th scope="col">How computational thinking helps</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Software development</td>
          <td>Breaks a large system into modules, removes unnecessary features, and plans logic before coding.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Data analysis</td>
          <td>Finds repeating trends, separates useful from irrelevant data, and automates repeated tasks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Machine learning</td>
          <td>Uses pattern recognition to classify data and abstraction to keep only useful features.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Database design</td>
          <td>Uses decomposition to separate entities and pattern recognition to avoid repeated structures.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network security</td>
          <td>Breaks security problems into roles, threats, and responses while designing clear defensive procedures.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      These concepts are strongest when used together. A good solution rarely depends on only one of
      them. For example, a database designer may decompose the system into entities and
      relationships, abstract away irrelevant detail, recognize repeated data patterns, and then design
      an algorithmic process for validation or querying.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B1.1.4"
    summary="Trace flowcharts by following the execution path carefully and recording each variable change, decision, and output."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Flowcharts are diagrammatic representations of algorithms. They help show input, processing,
      decisions, and output clearly enough that a reader can follow the logic and trace the result for
      given data.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-flowchart-symbols">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Symbol type</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical wording</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Terminator</td>
          <td>Shows the start or end of the algorithm</td>
          <td><code>START</code>, <code>END</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Input/Output</td>
          <td>Reads data or displays a result</td>
          <td><code>INPUT a</code>, <code>OUTPUT total</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Process</td>
          <td>Performs an assignment or calculation</td>
          <td><code>sum = a + b</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Decision</td>
          <td>Tests a condition and branches</td>
          <td><code>a &gt; b?</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Flowline</td>
          <td>Shows direction of execution</td>
          <td>Arrow between symbols</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Connector</td>
          <td>Continues a flow across the chart</td>
          <td>Linked symbol marker</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-tracing-a-flowchart">Worked example: tracing a flowchart</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine a flowchart that inputs two different numbers and outputs the larger value. The decision
      is whether <code>a &gt; b</code>. If the condition is true, the algorithm stores <code>a</code> as the
      maximum; otherwise it stores <code>b</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">a</th>
          <th scope="col">b</th>
          <th scope="col">max</th>
          <th scope="col">Decision</th>
          <th scope="col">Output</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Input values</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-3</td>
          <td>-</td>
          <td>-</td>
          <td>-</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Check condition</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-3</td>
          <td>-</td>
          <td>True</td>
          <td>-</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Assign maximum</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-3</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-</td>
          <td>-</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Display result</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-3</td>
          <td>7</td>
          <td>-</td>
          <td>The larger value is 7</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Tracing rule">
      <p>
        Label both branches of every decision clearly and record the outcome of the condition while
        tracing. A missing Yes/No branch makes the flowchart ambiguous and weakens the explanation.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
